# rlessie.msg - Madam Esther "Essie" Kowalski, the Gutter (The Den)
# Plain ASCII only: the English Fallout 2 fonts have no umlauts or typographic quotes.

# Greeting and weekly report
{100}{}{You're back. Sit down if you've got the time. The Gutter doesn't wait for you, but it counts your money.}
{101}{}{Last week we had }
{102}{}{ customers. After costs we cleared $}
{103}{}{. There's $}
{104}{}{ in the till.}

{105}{}{Which part? I'll tell you straight, whatever it is.}
{106}{}{Let's go over how the house is run.}
{107}{}{Business outside these walls.}
{108}{}{Outside the Den? Or outside this door? Both are dangerous.}
{109}{}{Something else.}
{110}{}{Give me what's in the till.}
{111}{}{Let's talk prices.}
{112}{}{Let's talk about what our people get.}
{113}{}{How are our people holding up?}
{114}{}{You said there's a problem.}
{115}{}{Later.}
{116}{}{Money?}
{117}{}{Let's talk about the house. What could we build?}
{118}{}{Let's talk about our people.}

# Payout
{120}{}{Here. $}
{121}{}{. Count it if you like. I counted it twice.}
{122}{}{There's nothing. The week cost more than it brought in. It happens. In the Den it happens a lot.}
{123}{}{Good. Go on.}
{124}{}{That's all.}

# Prices
{130}{}{The Den is poor, sweetheart. Charge too much here and the rooms go empty. Charge too little and you get the kind of customers nobody wants.}
{131}{}{Cheap. Quantity over quality.}
{132}{}{The usual prices.}
{133}{}{Charge more. We're better than the rest.}
{134}{}{Leave it as it is.}
{135}{}{Right now we take whatever people can still scrape together.}
{136}{}{Right now we charge the usual.}
{137}{}{Right now we charge more than the others.}
{138}{}{Right now we charge prices nobody in the Den can pay.}
{139}{}{As you like. Starting next week.}

# Staff share
{140}{}{Right now our people keep }
{141}{}{ percent of what they bring in. The rest belongs to the house. What do you want to change?}
{142}{}{A quarter is enough. They should be glad to have a roof.}
{143}{}{The usual. A third.}
{144}{}{Almost half. Whoever works well should live well.}
{145}{}{You can do that. Once. Then the good ones leave, and you're left with the ones who have nowhere else to go. You won't think about them after that, will you?}
{146}{}{That's how it's done in the Den. Nobody gets rich, nobody starves.}
{147}{}{Either you're smarter than the others, or you haven't been here long enough. They'll thank you for it. In their own way.}
{148}{}{Leave everything as it is.}

# Morale
{150}{}{They're laughing again. Not in front of the johns, they always do that. With each other.}
{151}{}{They work. They complain. That's a good sign, as long as they still do.}
{152}{}{They don't talk to me anymore. That's not a good sign.}
{153}{}{One of them ran off last night. She took nothing, not even her shoes. Think about what that means.}

# Crisis: the drug
{160}{}{One of ours is hooked on Jet. A week now. She's already stealing from the till, and soon she'll be stealing from the johns' pockets. Then she's dead, or we are.}
{161}{}{[Doctor] I'll look at her myself.}
{162}{}{Here's $200. Get her what she needs.}
{163}{}{Pay her in Jet instead of wages. At least she won't run.}
{164}{}{Throw her out.}
{165}{}{She screamed for three days. Now she's sleeping. Thank you. Nobody here forgets a thing like that.}
{166}{}{I'll take her to someone who knows about these things. I can't promise anything.}
{167}{}{I won't tell her that. You tell her yourself. And then watch how the others look at you afterward.}
{168}{}{She'll end up in the gutter. The real one. But fine, it's your house.}

# Other crises
{170}{}{Something happened that I can't handle alone.}
{171}{}{Here's $300. Take care of it.}
{172}{}{I'll deal with it later.}
{173}{}{Money doesn't fix everything. It fixes this.}
{181}{}{Soldiers in black armor. Drunk. They won't leave, and none of us can make them.}
{182}{}{Someone's hooked on Jet.}
{183}{}{They come and search the house. Not for the first time.}
{184}{}{A john beat one of ours half to death. He says he'll be back.}
{185}{}{Something's going around. Two are already down.}
{186}{}{Money's missing from the till. Not much. Not yet.}
{187}{}{Two are gone. Just gone.}
{188}{}{Metzger's men were here last night. They broke two doors and one of our girls' arms, and they left a message: next time they bring chains.}

# Metzger's retaliation (quiet war)
{174}{}{I'll deal with Metzger's men myself.}
{175}{}{Send word to the Rangers. They owe us.}
{176}{}{The next time Metzger's men came, you were waiting. Two of them won't be coming back. The Den will talk about it for a week.}
{177}{}{The Rangers put two men on the roof for a week. Metzger's boys took one look and went back to the Guild.}

{192}{}{[Debug] Take over the Gutter.}
{193}{}{[Debug] The Gutter is yours now. House class 1, three rooms, three people.}
{194}{}{[Debug] Show me the ending.}
{195}{}{[Debug] Add two empty rooms.}

# Look
{200}{}{A woman around fifty with a hard stare and too much rouge.}
{201}{}{Madam Esther Kowalski. Once at the Cat's Paw, now in the Gutter. She looks at you like she's putting a price on you.}

# Prologue "Essie's Debts"
{300}{}{If you're not from Metzger, sit down.}
{301}{}{I owe the Guild money. More than this dump will ever make. Every week Kolbe comes, Metzger's collector, and every week it's more.}
{302}{}{How much?}
{303}{}{Twelve hundred when it started. Fifty more every week. Metzger calls it interest. I call it a noose.}
{304}{}{I'll deal with Kolbe. In return, the house is mine afterward.}
{305}{}{The house is yours if you pull it off. And I stay and run it. That's how it was at the Cat's Paw: the names on the door change, the madam stays. Kolbe is in the back. He doesn't like to wait.}
{306}{}{Not my problem.}
{307}{}{I'll go see him.}
{310}{}{Kolbe's still here. The debt stands at $}
{311}{}{. Every day you wait, he's counting.}
{312}{}{I'm taking care of it.}

# Essie's reaction to the player's path (330 + path)
{331}{}{You paid. Just like that. I don't know if that was smart, but I won't forget it.}
{332}{}{Now Metzger gets his cut every week, forever. You leased us out to him. I hope you know what that means.}
{333}{}{Nobody's ever laid a hand on Kolbe. The Den talks about nothing else. Metzger will pay you back for it, but he'll respect you too.}
{334}{}{The debt note is gone? Then pray Metzger never counts again. He always counts again.}

# Essie's reaction to Metzger's offer (chains, act 1)
{350}{}{Metzger's men came in the night and turned the bolts on the cellar door. I heard every screw. There are two of them down there now. I'll run your house. I won't go down those stairs, and I won't count them.}
{351}{}{Kolbe told half the Den you said no to Metzger. Good. I've waited twenty years for somebody to say it to his face. Now watch your back. And mine.}
{352}{}{Go on.}

# Construction
{460}{}{Where do we start? The roof leaks, the beds creak, and the door won't hold up to a kick.}
{461}{}{The house itself and the furnishings.}
{462}{}{The business: security, staff, the books.}
{463}{}{Something you only get here in the Den.}
{464}{}{Which one?}
{465}{}{There's nothing to build right now, sweetheart.}
{466}{}{It costs $}
{467}{}{. There's $}
{468}{}{ in the till, and you're carrying $}
{469}{}{. Whatever the till can't cover comes out of your pocket.}
{470}{}{Build it.}
{471}{}{Not enough money for that. Back.}
{472}{}{We're already building: }
{473}{}{. Another }
{474}{}{ week(s). One thing at a time.}
{475}{}{Good. It'll be done in }
{476}{}{ week(s). Until then there's hammering all day, and half the customers stay away.}
{477}{}{Back.}

# Staff and recruiter
{480}{}{We have }
{481}{}{ rooms and }
{482}{}{ people to work them.}
{483}{}{Nobody is out looking for new faces.}
{484}{}{Our recruiter sits at Mom's and listens. The ones who want out of the Den come to us sooner or later. It's slow, but they stay.}
{485}{}{Our recruiter doesn't ask. Debts, Jet, a hand on the neck. The ones he brings in don't look at anyone.}
{486}{}{Find someone who talks people into it. ($40 a week, $50 a head)}
{487}{}{Find someone who doesn't ask. ($40 a week, $50 a head)}
{488}{}{Let the recruiter go.}
{489}{}{Back.}
{490}{}{I know a woman at Mom's who hears every sad story in the Den. She'll send us the ones who want out. Give it time.}
{491}{}{There's always someone in the Den who knows who owes what. I'll find him, because it's your house. Don't ask me to like it. And don't expect them to smile.}
{492}{}{Done. Nobody new is coming in for now.}
{493}{}{Every room is taken. The recruiter is just costing us money.}
{494}{}{And there are }
{495}{}{ in the cellar. I don't count them. I can't.}
{496}{}{Send word to Metzger. There's room in the cellar. ($}
{497}{}{ a head)}
{498}{}{Kolbe brings one the same night. Nobody in the house says a word.}

# The route south (chains, act 3, escape branch)
{600}{}{The route south.}
{601}{}{Somebody has to know where to take them. The Rangers are hiding somewhere in the NCR, but nobody in the Den knows where, and the ones who did are in the pens.}
{602}{}{[Perception] I've seen their marks. I know where to look.}
{603}{}{We can't hide anyone until there's a room in this house that nobody knows about. Build the hidden room first.}
{604}{}{They're on the road. We'll know by next week. Don't ask me to pray, I stopped doing that at the Cat's Paw.}
{605}{}{There are people waiting in the hidden room. So far we've gotten }
{606}{}{ group(s) through. Who takes them south this time?}
{607}{}{I'll take them myself. [}
{608}{}{% chance]}
{609}{}{Back.}
{610}{}{An old garage in Downtown, then. Nobody would look there. That's why they chose it. I'll tell the ones who come to us.}
{611}{}{They leave tonight, through the back, one at a time. If this goes wrong, Metzger won't just come for them.}

# Chains, acts 2-5 (Umsetzung 5)
{800}{}{Before you say anything. There's a girl in the cellar, behind the old boiler. Mara. She got out of one of Metzger's caravans three nights ago and ran the one direction nobody would look: toward the Guild. I gave her water and a blanket. That's all I've done, and it's already enough to hang me.}
{801}{}{What do you want me to do?}
{802}{}{Talk to her. Then decide, because Metzger's men are counting heads, and they'll keep counting until they come up one short. Whatever you decide, I'll have to live with it. So will she. Or she won't.}
{803}{}{I'll talk to her.}
{810}{}{My bag is packed. I watched them take her out, and I watched you count the money. Forty years in this trade and I never sold one of my own. I'm not starting now. Kolbe can have the keys. He's wanted them long enough.}
{811}{}{Goodbye, Essie.}
{820}{}{Metzger's men are asking about a runaway. Door to door. The cellar won't hide her from men who know what they're looking for. We need a room nobody knows about, or she has to leave the Den tonight.}
{821}{}{Build the hidden room.}
{830}{}{Lara sent word. Her people are ready. The Rangers you've been sending runaways to are asking the same question: when. Metzger doesn't know how many knives are pointed at his back. Not yet.}
{831}{}{Tonight. Tell them we go in.}
{832}{}{Not yet. We keep the route running.}
{833}{}{I need to think about it.}
{834}{}{Then it's done. Lara's people will be waiting near the Guild at dusk, with a Ranger squad behind them. They'll keep his guards busy. Metzger himself is yours. Don't come back until he's dead.}
{835}{}{Lara's people are waiting near the Guild. They won't wait forever, and neither will Metzger once he smells it. He's yours.}
{836}{}{Then we keep taking them out one at a time, and Metzger keeps hunting us one at a time. That's a war too. Just a quiet one. He'll hit back, you know. Sooner or later he always does.}

# The other towns (Umsetzung 6)
{620}{}{Where else could we open a house?}
{621}{}{Every town with money has a house. Every town without one is waiting for somebody like you. Which one do you want to hear about?}
{622}{}{New Reno.}
{623}{}{Redding.}
{624}{}{Vault City.}
{625}{}{The NCR.}
{626}{}{San Francisco.}
{627}{}{Enough for now.}
{628}{}{Tell me about another town.}
{630}{}{The Silver Garter, on Virgin Street, across from the Cat's Paw. A hotel from before the war. Empty since its last owner went into the ground at Golgotha. You'll need the deed, and you'll need one of the four families to bless it. Or you open without a blessing and see who comes knocking the first night. Take the servants' stairs at the side, somebody's always hanging around in there.}
{631}{}{Redding digs gold, and the miners spend it before the dust settles. Behind the sheds in the mining camp there's an old ore tunnel with stairs going down. Mayor Ascorti sells licenses for it. What he calls licenses.}
{632}{}{Vault City preaches by day and pays by night. In the Courtyard, behind Cassidy's, there's a widow with a boarding house and a very quiet cellar. The citizens come through the gate after dark. Nobody says a word, least of all the ones who vote on morality.}
{633}{}{The Republic licenses everything, even us. In the Bazaar, next to the Rawhide, there's a house with a number over the stairs: nine. It's waiting for a license and a sick room. Paperwork, fees, inspections. The most honest house on the coast. That isn't saying much.}
{634}{}{San Francisco. The Shi own the docks, and nothing happens down there they don't allow. There's a half-sunk ferry terminal nobody wants. The Shi's overseer decides who gets to keep a door open in it.}

